<CaseStudy title="Riker Bot" description="A small Discord automation project with optional AI advice and deliberate operational controls." category="Personal project / Automation & AI integration" role="Bot development & API integration" scope="Python · Discord · Scheduling · State management" next="/work/skeet-tracker/" nextLabel="Skeet Tracker">
  <a slot="links" class="button" href="https://github.com/jtdelosh-ops/RikerBot">View on GitHub ↗</a>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">01 / The idea</p><h2>A little personality.<br />A useful integration problem.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>Riker Bot brings Commander Riker-inspired personality to Discord through scheduled quotes, slash commands, and optional AI-generated responses.</p><p>The engineering interest is in the controls around that behavior: when to post, which channels to use, how to avoid repetitive content, and how to diagnose a failed send. The bot does not read ordinary server messages.</p></div></section>
  <div class="insight-grid"><article><span class="step-number">01</span><h3>Automate</h3><p>An hourly scheduler checks Eastern time, including daylight saving changes, and only posts between 9 a.m. and 9 p.m. It chooses from approved channels and adjusts posting probability by time and recent activity.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">02</span><h3>Integrate</h3><p>Discord commands connect to a local quote library and optional OpenAI advice. Generated scheduled remarks fall back to static quotes when AI is unavailable or fails.</p></article><article><span class="step-number">03</span><h3>Operate</h3><p>Per-user advice cooldowns limit API use. Recent quote history and posting state persist locally. Diagnostics check channel access and the permissions needed to send messages and embeds.</p></article></div>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">02 / Under the hood</p><h2>Small pieces with<br />clear responsibilities.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>A single Python application combines Discord event handling and command registration with a scheduled task. Quote content lives in a JSON file; lightweight state is saved through a temporary file and atomic replacement.</p><p>Recent-history filtering avoids repeated quotes when the library has alternatives. If every quote is in recent history, the bot can still select from the full library.</p><p>The advice command uses the OpenAI Responses API when configured. Its cooldown is tracked per user in memory. If AI is disabled it reports that directly; request failures receive a brief fallback response. Static quote fallback applies to scheduled generated remarks.</p></div></section>
  <div class="command-table"><div><code>/riker quote</code><p>Pick a configured quote while considering recent history.</p></div><div><code>/riker advice</code><p>Request a brief AI response, subject to a per-user cooldown.</p></div><div><code>/riker status</code><p>Inspect scheduling, channels, permissions, AI settings, and posting state.</p></div><div><code>/riker test_auto</code><p>Exercise the actual scheduled-send path, bypassing probability and quiet hours, with a server-wide one-minute cooldown.</p></div><div><code>/riker help</code><p>Show a private guide to the commands and posting behavior.</p></div></div>
  <section class="case-section"><div class="case-heading"><p class="eyebrow">03 / A detail that matters</p><h2>Test the path<br />that actually sends.</h2></div><div class="prose"><p>The scheduler and <code>/riker test_auto</code> share the same send function. That means the diagnostic command exercises channel resolution, permission validation, message construction, and Discord’s actual send operation.</p><p>A failed diagnostic can report the missing permission or channel problem. That is much more useful than a test that only proves the bot process is running.</p><p>In the code reviewed for this page, the test command is available to all users with a server-wide cooldown. The repository README’s administrator-only description has not caught up with that implementation.</p></div></section>
  <p class="source-note">Verified from <a href="https://github.com/jtdelosh-ops/RikerBot/blob/main/bot.py">bot.py</a> on September 10, 2026. Repository file revision: <code>7e7c510</code>.</p>
